<!--
  The three approved palettes.

  These are RAW VALUES, and their names say which palette they belong to on
  purpose — that is what they are. The *roles* live in `attrs.xml`, and the
  mapping from role to value lives in `themes.xml`. Nothing outside those two
  files may name a colour from here.

  Twelve values per palette are OWNER-APPROVED: the viewport, the base chrome,
  the floating material, the precision surface, the hairline, the two text
  weights, the accent, the selected-at-rest surface, and the warning / error /
  success verdicts. Everything else in a palette is derived from those twelve —
  a control fill lifted off the surface under it, a recessed field, a pressed
  state — and is authored as a NEIGHBOUR of an approved value rather than as a
  new opinion.

  TWO of the twelve were LIGHTENED, on instruction, because they were measured
  below the accessibility target on the surfaces they are actually drawn on.
  `*_text_secondary` carries field captions, section headings, slider labels and
  the active body's name; `*_text_error` carries every refusal the product
  reports. They measured 3.93 / 3.87 / 3.59 and 3.15 / 3.68 / 2.49 against the
  precision surface — a refusal a user cannot read is a refusal that did not
  happen. Every one now clears 4.5:1 against all six grounds either role is ever
  drawn on: the viewport, the chrome surface, the floating material, the
  precision surface, a control fill and a field well. The hue of each is kept —
  warm grey stays warm, the error stays red — so the appearance still reads as
  the same appearance. UILR1-11 measures this and will fail if it drifts back.

  The first three are dark, and they are the approved dark set: the product is
  a viewport-first modelling tool and every one of those grounds is chosen so a
  neutral clay render reads as lit. Warm Graphite leans red, Neutral Charcoal
  leans blue-grey, and Light Charcoal is the lightest dark ground. UI-PREF-R1
  (UI-OWNER-42) adds two LIGHT palettes, 04 and 05, DERIVED through the same
  roles and held to the same measured contrast: every text role clears 4.5:1
  on every ground it is drawn on (see artifacts/ui-pref-r1/CONTRAST.md). The
  three dark palettes are value-for-value what they were.

  One accent across all five, so the product still reads as itself in every
  appearance: the same #4C8FD6, meaning "primary commit" or "focused field" and
  nothing else. A SELECTION is a different, much quieter answer — see
  `attrs.xml` on why those are two roles and not one.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         PALETTE 01 — WARM GRAPHITE. The product default.

         A warm dark studio ground: red leads blue by five points, which is
         enough to keep a neutral clay body from looking cold and far short of
         brown.
         ===================================================================== -->
